  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Documenting API Keys**\n\nI\'m currently focused on documenting environment variables, specifically `VITE_MAPTILER_API_KEY=` and `VITE_MAP_STYLE_URL=` in the `.env.example` file. This aligns with the established environment variable best practices. I\'m also carefully re-reading the instructions to ensure I haven\'t overlooked any crucial details, particularly the explicit directive to avoid Google Maps.\n\n\n**Defining Map Requirements**\n\nI\'ve decided to proceed with MapLibre GL JS for the interactive map and MapTiler for the production-ready vector tiles and styles, since there isn\'t a map provider yet. GeoJSON will handle the threat/location data. Environment variables will keep the API keys secure. My design also outlines a \'Global Threat Intelligence\' dashboard section with a dark, professional, and minimal world map, ensuring a clean SOC aesthetic.\n\n\n**Visualizing Threat Data**\n\nI\'m now detailing the visual representation of threat data. Markers will dynamically change appearance based on threat severity, using a clear hierarchy from critical to low. I plan to avoid hardcoding colors by using centralized design tokens. Also, connection lines between threat events are on my mind; curved arcs will show direction between source and destination, with minimal animation.\n\n\n**Implementing Map Features**\n\nI\'m now integrating the map components and considering the existing routing. I plan to incorporate a concise legend that explains threat severity levels, also focusing on integrating key summary statistics, such as threat locations and critical events. I\'ll need to adapt and potentially mock the data for development, if it is not ready on the backend, using the ideal data structure described.\n\n\n**Integrating Map into Layout**\n\nI\'m now carefully examining the existing dashboard structure, specifically `SecurityOverviewPage.tsx`, to integrate the interactive map. I am paying close attention to the right-hand side\'s four-column layout, and I\'m looking at how I can best replace the existing static map with my \"Global Threat Intelligence\" section. I\'m focusing on user experience, and have started considering how to handle various states like loading, empty, and error scenarios. I\'m also planning the necessary UI controls.\n\n\n**Enhancing Dashboard Layout**\n\nI\'m now integrating crucial summary statistics above the interactive map, like Threat Locations and Critical Events, to provide at-a-glance insights. I\'m focusing on the `SecurityOverviewPage.tsx` structure, considering a full-width \"Global Threat Intelligence\" section featuring the interactive world map, comprehensive data, and filter controls. This should replace the existing static card. I am also planning for various scenarios where users might want to see specific map data directly within the sidebar or as a dedicated section.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 4
      nanos: 802294075
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
