  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Reviewing Map Features**\n\nI\'m now diving deep into the necessary map characteristics. Focusing on the WORLD MAP component, I\'m concentrating on achieving a responsive design with zoom and pan capabilities. The dark map style is a definite requirement, and I\'m aiming for country and boundary visibility that\'s clear but not distracting.\n\n\n**Defining Threat Markers**\n\nI\'ve moved on to defining the threat markers, considering the data points to be included: geolocation, IP, risk scores, and threat levels. I\'m focusing on visual representation, especially color-coding based on severity, and I\'m very keen on utilizing design tokens for a consistent and maintainable implementation. I\'m thinking I\'ll represent the most critical threats with strong visual emphasis, like rose/red colors, while lower threats get more subtle visual cues.\n\n\n**Implementing Marker Functionality**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the marker behavior, and it\'s all about effective visualization. I\'ve considered animation nuances, ensuring subtle visual cues for hovering and selection, with an emphasis on avoiding anything overwhelming. Marker clustering is next: I\'m planning to handle overlapping events with clusters, zoom interactions, and efficient rendering using MapLibre\'s native capabilities. The goal is to provide a clean and informative user experience.\n\n\n**Designing Map Controls**\n\nNow I\'m focusing on user interactions and map controls. A compact legend is crucial for explaining threat severity and connection arcs. Summary stats for threat locations, affected countries, malicious IPs, and critical events will be above or beside the map. I\'m prioritizing zoom controls, a reset button, and a full-screen toggle, alongside severity filters and IP/country search. I\'ll also add a connection arc toggle. And of course, loading, empty, and error states are essential.\n\n\n**Analyzing Data Source**\n\nI\'m now digging into the data structure to create a data adapter. The `casesStore` seems to be the place, containing both cases and investigations. I\'m focusing on the `geolocation` data within the `investigations` and just confirmed that each entry of the `GeoLocationSummary` in `src/contracts/backend.ts` contains vital country and geolocation information, including ip, country code and latitude and longitude!\n\n\n**Adapting Data Structures**\n\nNow I\'m focusing on the data adapter, given the data contract requirement. I\'ll translate the real case and investigation data into normalized `ThreatLocationEvent` records, ensuring the adapter resolves coordinates using a well-defined coordinates dictionary where necessary, and creating a safe fallback/mock data solution for development preview if coordinates are unavailable. I\'m keen on establishing the adapter as the vital intermediary to produce clean data for GeoJSON and MapLibre layers.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 6
      nanos: 386931121
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
