  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Forensic Data**\n\nOkay, I\'m digging into this forensic data. Right now, I\'m looking at `casesStore`. I\'ve got a couple of IP addresses associated with specific cases: `A2291` points to 185.220.101.5, which is Germany, with the recipient being `user@company.com` in India or the US. I also have `A2290`, pointing to India. Still processing...\n\n\n**Mapping Threat Locations**\n\nI\'ve expanded the IP analysis significantly. I\'ve cataloged several new entries: `A2288` from Indore, India; `A2287` from Moscow, Russia; `ASH-2026-9012` from Frankfurt, Germany; `ASH-2026-9015` from Mumbai, India; `ASH-2026-9018` from Amsterdam, Netherlands; `ASH-2026-9022` from San Francisco, USA; `ASH-2026-9025` from Bucharest, Romania and `ASH-2026-9031` from London, UK. Now I\'m shifting focus to designing the threat map architecture and defining the data types for its components.\n\n\n**Defining Threat Map Data**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on defining the data structures that will drive the threat map. I\'ve got the `Threat` interface mapped out, including attributes like `count`, `source`, `lastSeen`, and more. I\'m also planning the GeoJSON types for map features, alongside the map filter and statistics components like severity and search parameters. I\'m making sure to define realistic geolocation with the coordinates lookup.\n\n\n**Expanding Geolocation Data**\n\nI\'m expanding the geolocation data, adding cities in Russia, Netherlands, Romania, China, Brazil, UK, France, Singapore, Japan, Australia, and Canada, as well as a fallback dictionary for country centers. I\'m ensuring latitude and longitude are clamped to valid ranges during parsing.\n\n\\n\\n\n\nI\'ve adapted the data adapter to process cases and investigations, mapping geolocation, origin data, and transit hops. Threat levels are now being calculated based on risk scores and verdicts, categorizing threats as critical, high, medium, or low. I am generating ThreatLocationEvents.\n\n\n**Generating Threat Map Visuals**\n\nNow, I\'m working on the threat map visualization itself. I\'m producing GeoJSON FeatureCollections, with point features detailed with properties like IP address, country, threat level, and event count. Additionally, I\'m generating curved connection arcs to connect sources and destinations using interpolation, generating summaries on threat location counts, countries affected, malicious IPs, and critical events. I\'ve also implemented error-handling code and fallbacks in case map tile services are unavailable.\n\n\n**Refining Threat Visualization**\n\nMy focus is now on refining the map\'s visual representation. I am using MapLibre circle layers, implementing color expressions to show threat levels with increasing circle radii based on risk scores. I\'m also implementing a pulse ring for the stroke layer. Finally, I\'m setting up native clustering, and a connection arc layer for enhanced data presentation.\n\n\n**Designing Threat Map Popup**\n\nI\'m now implementing a high-fidelity SOC threat card. It will include defanged IPs, risk scores, location details (city, country, flag), network information (ASN, ISP), metadata (source, event count, last seen), related case details, and a primary \"View Evidence\" action that links to the case. It will also have a close button and responsive positioning.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 7
      nanos: 71100129
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
