    session_summary: "### 1. Outstanding User Requests\n- **Status: COMPLETED.** The user requested to \"Finish what you were doing\" following an unexpected error (which manifested as a linting/compilation failure in `GlobalThreatMap.tsx` due to incorrect `maplibre-gl` imports and an unhandled Promise in `getClusterExpansionZoom`). This has been resolved.\n\n### 2. User Knowledge\n- **Preferences:** The user desires a \"dark cybersecurity SOC aesthetic\" for the threat map, utilizing high-contrast tokens (Rose-500, Orange-500, Amber-500, Sky-400).\n- **Architecture:** The application uses a custom store (`cases-store.ts`) with `useSyncExternalStore` for state management.\n- **Environment:** The map configuration relies on `VITE_MAPTILER_API_KEY` and `VITE_MAP_STYLE_URL` defined in `/.env.example`.\n- **Scope:** The user requested a complete feature set for a Global Threat Intelligence Map, including data adaptation, visualization, and UI components.\n\n### 3. Work Accomplished\n- **Feature Implementation:** Created a comprehensive `threat-map` feature set in `/src/features/dashboard/threat-map/` including:\n    - `types.ts`: Data contracts for threats and GeoJSON.\n    - `coordinatesLookup.ts`: Geolocation resolver with fallbacks.\n    - `mapDataAdapter.ts`: Logic to convert case telemetry into GeoJSON features (points and arcs).\n    - `mapStyles.ts`: MapLibre layer definitions, color tokens, and style URL management.\n    - `ThreatPopup.tsx`, `MapLegend.tsx`, `MapControls.tsx`, `ThreatMapStats.tsx`: UI components for the map.\n    - `GlobalThreatMap.tsx`: Main map component integrating MapLibre GL.\n- **Bug Fixes:**\n    - Fixed `maplibre-gl` imports in `GlobalThreatMap.tsx` to use `import * as maplibregl` for compatibility.\n    - Resolved `TS2554` error by updating `getClusterExpansionZoom` to handle the returned `Promise` correctly.\n- **Configuration:** Updated `/.env.example` to support custom map styles and MapTiler API keys.\n\n### 4. Model Knowledge\n- **MapLibre GL:**\n    - `getClusterExpansionZoom` is asynchronous and returns a `Promise<number | null>`. It must be handled with `.then()`/`.catch()` or `await`.\n    - `import * as maplibregl` is required for proper type resolution in this environment.\n    - `GeoJSONSource` must be cast when accessed via `map.getSource()`.\n- **State Management:** The `cases-store.ts` uses `useSyncExternalStore` and exports a `useCases` hook.\n- **Failures:** Initial attempts to use named imports for `maplibre-gl` caused runtime issues; `* as` solved this. Direct callback usage for `getClusterExpansionZoom` failed due to type mismatches (expected Promise).\n\n### 5. Files and Code\n- **Edited Files:**\n    - `/.env.example`: Added map configuration variables.\n    - `/src/features/dashboard/threat-map/GlobalThreatMap.tsx`: Fixed imports and cluster zoom promise handling.\n- **Created Files:**\n    - `/src/features/dashboard/threat-map/types.ts`\n    - `/src/features/dashboard/threat-map/coordinatesLookup.ts`\n    - `/src/features/dashboard/threat-map/mapDataAdapter.ts`\n    - `/src/features/dashboard/threat-map/mapStyles.ts`\n    - `/src/features/dashboard/threat-map/ThreatPopup.tsx`\n    - `/src/features/dashboard/threat-map/MapLegend.tsx`\n    - `/src/features/dashboard/threat-map/MapControls.tsx`\n    - `/src/features/dashboard/threat-map/ThreatMapStats.tsx`\n    - `/src/features/dashboard/threat-map/GlobalThreatMap.tsx`\n- **Viewed Files:**\n    - `/src/stores/cases-store.ts`: Examined for store structure and hooks.\n    - `/node_modules/maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.d.ts`: Checked for type definitions.\n    - `/src/App.tsx`: Checked for routing structure.\n    - `/src/features/dashboard/SecurityOverviewPage.tsx`: Inspected existing dashboard layout.\n    - `/src/components/layout/AppShell.tsx`: Inspected sidebar/layout structure.\n\n### 6. Current Work and Next Steps\n- **Status:** The `GlobalThreatMap` feature is fully implemented and linted.\n- **Next Steps:** The next agent should integrate the `GlobalThreatMap` component into the `SecurityOverviewPage.tsx` or a dedicated dashboard view. The component is ready to be imported and rendered. No further implementation of the map feature itself is required."
    included_step_index_start: 91
    included_step_index_end: 153
    conversation_log_uris: "file:///.aistudio/artifacts/brain/46cbd628-708b-47bd-9306-1c407fe4adfa/.system_generated/logs/transcript.jsonl"
    user_requests: "Here is the full initial file tree for the application:\n/.env.example\n/.gitignore\n/ASHIELDER_FRONTEND_ARCHITECTURE.md\n/ASHIELDER_FRONTEND_IMPLEMENTATION_AUDIT.md\n/ASHIELDER_FRONTEND_PRODUCT_SPEC.md\n/ASHIELDER_FRONTEND_WIREFRAME_SPEC.md\n/bun.lock\n/index.html\n/metadata.json\n/package.json\n/public/assets/aistudio/.gitignore\n/src/App.tsx\n/src/api/client.ts\n/src/api/endpoints.ts\n/src/api/errors.ts\n/src/components/feedback/ErrorBoundary.tsx\n/src/components/feedback/NotFoundPage.tsx\n/src/components/layout/AppShell.tsx\n/src/components/layout/AppTopBar.tsx\n/src/components/layout/Breadcrumbs.tsx\n/src/components/layout/GlobalSearchModal.tsx\n/src/components/layout/GlobalStatusBar.tsx\n/src/components/layout/NavigationRail.tsx\n/src/components/layout/Sidebar.tsx\n/src/components/layout/ToastContainer.tsx\n/src/components/ui/Badge.tsx\n/src/components/ui/Button.tsx\n/src/components/ui/Card.tsx\n/src/components/ui/EvidenceChip.tsx\n/src/components/ui/Modal.tsx\n/src/components/ui/Tooltip.tsx\n/src/contracts/backend.ts\n/src/domain/case.ts\n/src/domain/evidence.ts\n/src/domain/hop.ts\n/src/domain/mappers/case-mapper.ts\n/src/domain/mappers/evidence-mapper.ts\n/src/domain/mappers/hop-mapper.ts\n/src/features/admin/HealthPlaceholder.tsx\n/src/features/auth/LoginPage.tsx\n/src/features/case/CaseDetailPage.tsx\n/src/features/case/CasePlaceholder.tsx\n/src/features/dashboard/SecurityOverviewPage.tsx\n/src/features/hunting/HuntingPlaceholder.tsx\n/src/features/inbox/CaseInboxPage.tsx\n/src/features/inbox/CaseTableRow.tsx\n/src/features/inbox/InboxPlaceholder.tsx\n/src/features/inbox/QuickPeekDrawer.tsx\n/src/features/ingest/DropzonePage.tsx\n/src/features/ingest/IngestPlaceholder.tsx\n/src/features/ingest/UploadProgressModal.tsx\n/src/features/settings/SettingsPlaceholder.tsx\n/src/index.css\n/src/lib/defang.ts\n/src/lib/formatters.ts\n/src/lib/sanitization.ts\n/src/lib/utils.ts\n/src/main.tsx\n/src/stores/auth-store.ts\n/src/stores/cases-store.ts\n/src/stores/ui-store.ts\n/src/tests/unit/foundations.test.ts\n/src/tests/unit/shell.test.ts\n/src/vite-env.d.ts\n/tsconfig.json\n/vite.config.ts"
    user_requests: "You are a Senior Frontend Engineer + UI/UX Engineer working on the existing ASHielder cybersecurity platform.\n\nYour task is to integrate a professional interactive WORLD THREAT MAP into the existing ASHielder frontend.\n\nIMPORTANT:\n\n* Inspect the existing repository and frontend architecture first.\n* Do NOT rewrite the application.\n* Do NOT modify backend logic, APIs, database schema, authentication, ML models, threat-analysis logic, or existing business logic.\n* Preserve all existing frontend/backend contracts.\n* Reuse the project\'s existing design system, components, spacing, typography, icons, and theme wherever possible.\n* The new map must feel like a native part of ASHielder, not a separately designed component.\n\nTECHNICAL DECISION:\n\nDo NOT use Google Maps.\n\nUse:\n\n* MapLibre GL JS for the interactive map.\n* A production-appropriate vector tile/style provider such as MapTiler if the project does not already have a map provider.\n* GeoJSON for threat/location data.\n* Use environment variables for map provider/API keys.\n* Never expose threat-intelligence API secrets directly in frontend code.\n\nFIRST:\nInspect:\n\n1. Frontend framework and version.\n2. Current routing/layout structure.\n3. Existing dashboard components.\n4. Existing theme/colors.\n5. Existing API/data-fetching layer.\n6. Package manager and build system.\n7. Whether any map/chart library already exists.\n8. Existing threat/IP/geolocation data structures.\n\nThen implement the map using the project\'s current architecture.\n\nCORE UI:\n\nCreate a dashboard section called:\n\n\"Global Threat Intelligence\"\n\nThe section should contain a large interactive world map designed specifically for a cybersecurity/SOC dashboard.\n\nVisual direction:\n\n* Dark cybersecurity dashboard.\n* Professional and minimal.\n* Avoid flashy gaming-style UI.\n* Subtle world geography.\n* Clear country boundaries.\n* Threat locations should stand out.\n* Proper visual hierarchy.\n* Responsive layout.\n* Clean enterprise SOC aesthetic.\n\nMAP FEATURES:\n\n1. WORLD MAP\n\n* Full world map view.\n* Zoom and pan.\n* Responsive.\n* Dark map style.\n* Countries and major geographic boundaries visible but subtle.\n\n2. THREAT MARKERS\n   Display IP/geolocation events as map markers.\n\nEach marker should support:\n\n* latitude\n* longitude\n* IP address\n* country\n* city\n* ASN\n* organization/ISP if available\n* risk score\n* threat level\n* event count\n\nMarker appearance should be based on threat severity.\n\nExample:\n\n* Critical \342\206\222 strongest visual emphasis\n* High \342\206\222 strong emphasis\n* Medium \342\206\222 moderate\n* Low \342\206\222 subtle\n\nDo not hard-code colors throughout the component. Use centralized theme/design tokens if the project already has them.\n\n3. THREAT CONNECTIONS\n   When source and destination coordinates are available, draw curved connection lines/arcs between them.\n\nExample:\n\nAttacker IP\n\342\206\223\nGermany \342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\206\222 India\n\342\206\223\nsuspicious email\n\nThe connections should clearly communicate direction.\n\nAvoid excessive animation.\n\n4. MARKER CLUSTERING\n   When many events overlap:\n\n* Cluster nearby markers.\n* Show cluster count.\n* Zoom into cluster on click.\n* Avoid hundreds of individually rendered DOM elements.\n\nUse MapLibre\'s native source/layer capabilities where appropriate.\n\n5. POPUP / DETAILS\n\nClicking a threat marker should open a professional popup/card containing:\n\nIP Address\nLocation\nCountry\nCity\nASN\nISP/Organization\nRisk Score\nThreat Level\nEvent Count\nSource\nLast Seen\n\nAdd a clear action such as:\n\n\"View Evidence\"\n\nIf the existing application has a threat investigation route/page, connect to it using the existing routing system.\n\nDo not create fake routes.\n\n6. MAP LEGEND\n\nAdd a compact legend explaining threat severity.\n\nExample:\n\nCritical\nHigh\nMedium\nLow\n\nKeep it visually subtle and consistent with the existing dashboard.\n\n7. SUMMARY STATISTICS\n\nAbove or beside the map, show useful existing-data statistics such as:\n\nThreat Locations\nCountries Affected\nMalicious IPs\nCritical Events\n\nIMPORTANT:\nUse real data from the existing frontend/backend wherever available.\n\nDo NOT invent fake statistics when real data exists.\n\nIf the backend currently does not provide these fields, create a clean frontend data adapter with safe fallback/mock data ONLY for development preview, clearly isolated from production data flow.\n\nDATA CONTRACT:\n\nPrefer a normalized structure like:\n\n{\n\"ip\": \"185.x.x.x\",\n\"lat\": 50.1109,\n\"lon\": 8.6821,\n\"country\": \"Germany\",\n\"city\": \"Frankfurt\",\n\"asn\": \"AS12345\",\n\"organization\": \"Example ISP\",\n\"risk_score\": 92,\n\"threat_level\": \"critical\",\n\"event_count\": 12,\n\"source\": \"email_header\",\n\"last_seen\": \"2026-09-07T12:00:00Z\"\n}\n\nDo NOT force the backend to use this exact structure if an existing API already has a different contract.\n\nInstead create a frontend adapter:\n\nexisting API response\n\342\206\223\nmap data adapter\n\342\206\223\nGeoJSON\n\342\206\223\nMapLibre layers\n\nPERFORMANCE:\n\nDesign for potentially hundreds/thousands of threat events.\n\nRequirements:\n\n* Prefer GeoJSON/vector sources + MapLibre layers over thousands of React DOM markers.\n* Cluster where appropriate.\n* Avoid unnecessary React re-renders.\n* Do not recreate the map instance on every state update.\n* Properly clean up map/listeners on component unmount.\n* Keep map data transformation separate from rendering.\n* Do not block the main UI thread with expensive processing.\n\nSECURITY:\n\n* Never put private threat-intelligence API keys in client-side source code.\n* Map provider public/client-safe key may use environment variables according to provider rules.\n* Do not expose internal backend credentials.\n* Sanitize any values displayed in popups.\n* Do not trust arbitrary HTML from API responses.\n\nRESPONSIVENESS:\n\nDesktop:\n\n* Large map area.\n* Details/summary integrated naturally.\n\nTablet:\n\n* Reduced side-panel width.\n\nMobile:\n\n* Map remains usable.\n* Popup becomes a bottom sheet/card.\n* Controls remain accessible.\n\nUX DETAILS:\n\nAdd:\n\n* zoom controls\n* reset/home map control\n* optional fullscreen control if compatible with existing design\n* loading state\n* empty state\n* error state\n\nExample empty state:\n\n\"No geolocation data available for this investigation.\"\n\nDo not show a blank map without context.\n\nERROR HANDLING:\n\nHandle:\n\n* map provider failure\n* missing coordinates\n* invalid coordinates\n* API failure\n* empty threat dataset\n* malformed threat records\n\nInvalid coordinates must not break the map.\n\nARCHITECTURE:\n\nKeep components modular.\n\nRecommended structure if compatible with the existing project:\n\ncomponents/\nGlobalThreatMap/\nGlobalThreatMap\nMapLayers\nThreatPopup\nMapLegend\nMapControls\nmapDataAdapter\n\nUse the project\'s existing naming conventions instead of blindly copying this structure.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION PROCESS:\n\n1. Inspect repository.\n2. Identify existing dashboard page.\n3. Identify existing threat/geolocation data.\n4. Identify existing design tokens/theme.\n5. Install only the minimum required MapLibre dependency.\n6. Implement map component.\n7. Connect real API data.\n8. Convert threat events into GeoJSON.\n9. Add layers, markers, clusters, and connections.\n10. Add popup/details interaction.\n11. Add loading/error/empty states.\n12. Verify responsiveness.\n13. Verify build and lint/type-check.\n14. Fix any integration issues.\n\nIMPORTANT UI RULE:\n\nDo not make the map look like generic Google Maps.\n\nASHielder is a cybersecurity forensic intelligence platform, so the map should visually communicate:\n\n\"Where are these threats coming from?\"\n\nwhile still remaining sober, professional, and readable.\n\nDO NOT:\n\n* add unnecessary 3D globe effects\n* add excessive particle effects\n* add distracting animations\n* add random fake cyber graphics\n* introduce a new UI framework\n* rewrite existing components unnecessarily\n* duplicate API calls\n* hard-code production threat data\n* modify backend contracts without necessity\n\nFINAL OUTPUT:\n\nAfter implementation:\n\n1. Summarize exactly which files were created/modified.\n2. Mention the MapLibre package/provider used.\n3. Explain how existing threat data is mapped to GeoJSON.\n4. Explain the API/data flow.\n5. List any environment variables required.\n6. Report build/lint/type-check status.\n7. Clearly mention any assumptions or places where existing backend data was unavailable.\n\nThe final result must be production-quality, visually consistent with ASHielder, performant, and easy for another engineer to maintain.\nApply style changes to the selected element(s).\n[AIS_METADATA_SECTION_START]\nCSS selector 1: div#root:nth-of-type(1) > div:nth-of-type(1) > div:nth-of-type(1) > main:nth-of-type(1) > div:nth-of-type(1) > div:nth-of-type(2) > div:nth-of-type(2) > div:nth-of-type(2)\nCSS 1: {\n\n}\n[AIS_METADATA_SECTION_END]"
    user_requests: "There was an unexpected error. Finish what you were doing."
  }
